## Replay storage: what does the buffer actually need to contain? (2026-09)

ExRD stores 11,000 preprocessed images, which is 6.62 GB (224x224x3 fp32 =
602 kB each). No MTIL paper reports this number. This phase asks what that
buffer has to hold and what shrinking it costs. Branch: `feature-replay`.

### Motivation: gradient routing across CLIP's two towers

Verified in code (`src/models/training.py`, `phase3/losses_phase3.py`):

| Loss term | Image encoder | Text encoder (old classes) |
|---|---|---|
| L_zscl | yes | no (teacher text embeddings under `no_grad`) |
| L_RD | yes | no (same) |
| L2 | no | no |
| L_rep (replay CE) | yes | **yes** |

Excluding current-task CE, the image encoder has **three** constraints and the
text encoder for old classes has **one**, which is replay. So replay's
image-side gradient is largely redundant and its text-side gradient is unique.
Storing images is how the redundant half is bought. Not found in the
literature; this is the argument feature replay tests.

Note: `--text_loss` transposes the logits (softmax direction). It does **not**
route gradient into the text encoder. Easy to misread.

### Feature replay (v0) and the drift-adaptation ladder

Store the final 512-d embedding (1.02 kB) instead of the image. 588x smaller.
`src/feature_replay_buffer.py`, `src/feature_adaptation.py`,
`--replay_storage feature`, `--feature_adapt {none,sdc,lp,linear,mlp}`,
`--feature_adapt_anchors {image,text,both}`.

| Run | Avg | Last | Transfer [IN] | Transfer [UT] | Buffer |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| ExRD / v4 (pixel) | 77.17 | 86.17 | 68.37 | 69.18 | 6.6 GB |
| featrep v0 (no adaptation) | 76.08 | 84.79 | 68.12 | 68.81 | 11 MB |
| featrep lp_both | 76.23 | 84.89 | 67.95 | 68.99 | 11 MB |
| featrep lp_text | 76.06 | 84.73 | 68.05 | 68.78 | 11 MB |
| featrep sdc_image | 76.20 | 84.65 | 67.90 | 68.88 | 11 MB |

`Transfer [IN]` = held-out ImageNet column (project convention).
`Transfer [UT]` = upper triangle (ZSCL/DIKI/BCL/AFA convention). External
comparisons must use [UT]. v4 [UT] = **69.18**, previously unverified.

**The penalty is one task.** Per-task Last, ExRD vs featrep v0: Aircraft
54.52 -> 44.55 (**-9.97**), CIFAR100 -2.24, DTD -1.33, Cars -0.91,
Caltech -0.81, EuroSAT and MNIST slightly *positive*, rest within 0.6.
Aircraft alone is **65.8%** of the 1.38-point gap; Aircraft + CIFAR100 is
**80.6%**. Damage scales with task age, which is the drift signature.

**Drift correction failed.** All four variants lie within 0.24 points on Last.
On Aircraft the ordering was **image > both > text ~ none** (sdc_image +2.19,
lp_both +1.68, lp_text +0.30). The text-anchor arm was the intended novelty
claim and it did not beat image anchors. Likely cause is CLIP's modality gap:
a drift vector measured at a text embedding does not transfer to image
features. Partly anticipated by SeGP-CL (arXiv 2603.12055, 2026).

### REMIND port to CLIP ViT-B/16

Hayes et al., ECCV 2020 (arXiv 1910.02509). Store PQ-compressed mid-network
token grids instead of final embeddings, so replayed samples keep a real
gradient path, and freeze everything below the tap so stored activations
cannot go stale. New: `src/product_quantizer.py` (201 lines, k-means PQ written
from scratch to avoid a faiss dependency), `src/remind_buffer.py` (493 lines,
split forward pass + freeze + buffer). Modified: one dispatch entry and one
freeze call in `trainer_phase3.py`, one loss function in `losses_phase3.py`,
six flags. **No ExRD loss term, optimizer or schedule was changed**, so pixel
runs stay bit-identical and every row is single-factor.

Five places REMIND's assumptions are false for CLIP:

| | Mismatch | REMIND assumes | MTIL/CLIP reality |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | one codebook | single-domain stream, fit once on task 0 | a codebook fit on Aircraft was quantizing MNIST and EuroSAT |
| 2 | even variance | CNN feature maps | residual streams have outlier channels that dominate any PQ subspace |
| 3 | a natural seam | CNN low-to-high hierarchy | every ViT block is the same full-width update; block 6 was a guess |
| 4 | a plastic linear head | head trains continuously, never stale | CLIP's "head" is the text encoder, which drifts and is constrained only by L_rep |
| 5 | cheap compression | sparse low-rank feature maps | ViT tokens are near full-rank, so the same bits buy far less fidelity |

Fixes implemented: per-task codebooks (`--remind_shared_codebook` to disable)
and per-channel standardisation (`--remind_no_standardize`). Diagnostic arms
added: `--remind_no_pq` (fp16 token oracle) and `--remind_no_freeze`.

### Results: the bit budget is what matters

PQ sweep at tap block 6, everything else fixed (jobs 22635629/32/34, 2026-09-25):

| m | Bytes/exemplar | Buffer | Error floor 2^-R | Last | Transfer [UT] |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 32 | 6.3 kB | 69 MB | 0.794 | 83.66 | 68.96 |
| 64 | 12.6 kB | 139 MB | 0.630 | 84.72 | 69.10 |
| 128 | 25.2 kB | 277 MB | 0.397 | **85.88** | **69.10** |
| pixel | 602 kB | 6.6 GB | 0 | 86.17 | 69.18 |

**Last increases monotonically with the bit budget and tracks the
rate-distortion bound.** At m=128, REMIND is within **0.29 Last and 0.08
Transfer of pixel replay at 24x less storage**. This is the headline result of
the phase.

**Crossover.** Mid-network replay does not earn its storage until roughly
140 MB. Below that, storing the final embedding at 11 MB is better:
m=32 (69 MB, 83.66) and m=64 (139 MB, 84.72) are both dominated by featrep v0
(11 MB, 84.79). Pareto frontier is featrep v0, REMIND m=128, pixel.

**Tap depth also helps.** Block 2 vs block 6 at m=32: **84.29 vs 83.66**
(+0.63). Fewer frozen blocks means less lost plasticity. In a ViT the token
grid is the same size at every depth, so unlike a CNN there is no storage
penalty for tapping shallow. Next run is LAYER=2 with PQ_M=128.

**Freeze trade.** REMIND m=32 vs featrep v0 per task: Aircraft +4.47 (drift
prevention working), StanfordCars -2.70 and EuroSAT -2.02 (plasticity lost).
Nets to roughly zero at that bit budget.

### Claims corrected during this phase

- "Replay fidelity is not the binding constraint" (argued from REMIND matching
  featrep at 0.58 reconstruction error). **Refuted by the m-sweep.** The
  rate-distortion analysis was right about the physics; the inference that a
  higher bit budget was not worth pursuing was wrong, and it delayed the sweep.
- Per-channel standardisation was described as whitening. It is not. Whitening
  decorrelates, which is what contiguous-subspace PQ needs; standardisation
  only equalises per-channel variance. Measured effect on the 11-task run was
  **negative** (84.62 -> 83.66 at m=32).
- `codebook_nbytes` assumed fp16 while `fit` stores fp32, under-reporting every
  per-task codebook by half. Corrected storage at m=32 is 78.0 MB, not 73.7 MB
  (12.6% codebook overhead, not 6.3%).

### Open issues

1. **Task-0 divergence, unexplained.** Aircraft's diagonal must be identical
   across runs: the buffer is empty, so storage mode cannot matter yet. ExRD
   and REMIND(m=32, shared codebook) both give 52.48 exactly. Every
   `--replay_storage feature` run diverges (52.03 to 53.23, spread 1.20), and
   the newer REMIND run gives 52.87. No `--seed` differs between the scripts.
   The adaptation ladder's 0.24-point spread is smaller than this artefact, so
   that null result is suggestive but not cleanly measured.
2. **L_RD relocation never measured on Last.** Under feature storage, L_RD
   switches to current-task images (`rd_source=current`). Control C1b validated
   this on Transfer only (66.66 vs 66.72). Its effect on Last is unknown and
   could account for a meaningful share of the 1.38-point gap.
3. **`--remind_no_freeze` has never actually run.** The Sep 27 submissions
   predate the launcher passthrough, so `REMIND_EXTRA` was ignored; the depth-2
   result above still has blocks 0-1 frozen.
4. **No feature-level augmentation.** REMIND applies random resized crops and
   manifold mixup to reconstructed tensors; the port applies none. The 196
   patch tokens lie on a 14x14 grid, so the crop has a faithful analogue.
5. **Exemplar count, not bytes, is the fixed quantity.** The benchmark pins the
   buffer at 11,000 exemplars, so compression can only lose. At equal *bytes*
   (6.6 GB) REMIND would hold over 1M exemplars. Worth stating as framing even
   though prior selection results suggest saturation well below 11k.
6. Single seed throughout.
